{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "moves-onroad",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 steps 7c' and 7d: the onroad domain CATALOGUE, reading the DEFAULT DATABASE. It declares the 40 default-database tables `onroad/model.esm` reads and mounts that model; a per-fixture document in `runspecs/` mounts THIS and supplies its RunSpec's selections and its oracle, which are the only things that vary between runs.\n\nIT ALSO DECLARES ONE METAPARAMETER, and only as a relay: `bindings` closes a metaparameter the REFERENCED document declares and a mounting document can offer only an expression over its OWN (docs/esm-conventions.md §49.1), so `n_outputPolProcess` -- how many of a run's selected pollutant-processes reach MOVESOutput -- is declared here as well and passed down. `runspecs/expand-day.esm` sets it to 1 where `runspecs/process-tirewear.esm` leaves it at 3.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IN THE MODEL, and it is three things rather than a re-pointing. An execution database is the default database already cut to the run, so a document written against one can leave its selections unsaid, and this one did. (1) `link`: summing a one-row table is the same as reading it, and the national Link has 22,610 rows, so the four run-level geography columns now join on the runspec's county and road type. (2) `sourceUseTypePhysicsMapping`: MOVES synthesises it by renaming one column of `sourceUseTypePhysics` and adding two this document never reads, so the document reads the real table. (3) `evSalesFraction`: MOVES computes it at execution time from four default tables the document already ingests, so it is an equation here (`svp_salesWeight`, `coh_salesAllFuels`, `coh_salesElectric`) rather than an input.\n\nEVERY OTHER TABLE IS A DROP-IN, and that is measured rather than assumed: swapping the national file in one table at a time and re-emitting gives byte-identical output for 36 of the 37 that exist in both, and `link` is the one that does not. All 38 together give the same 375 rows as the snapshot-fed catalogue. `emissionRateAdjustment` is the fortieth source and joined on 2026-09-20 (PLAN.md §7.3.9); it does not disturb that measurement, because no cohort of source type 21 matches a row of it and `runspecs/process-tirewear.esm` emits the same 375 rows with it read.\n\nIT DOES NOT LOAD ALONE, by design and like `onroad/model.esm`: it names no runspec, so `esm validate` on it reports the ten sources it does not declare. The per-fixture document is the complete one. There is no channel for a mounting document to OVERRIDE a mounted one's `data_sources` (a `SubsystemRef` carries `bindings`, `model`, `index_set_rename` and `expression_template_imports`, and nothing else), so a domain document that shipped a default runspec could not have it replaced -- which is why the runspec is the fixture's and not this file's.\n\nNO `float_columns`, WHERE THE SNAPSHOT CATALOGUE NEEDS 43 OF THEM OVER 27 TABLES. The snapshot writes every numeric column as decimal TEXT and the reader has to be told which to parse; the default database's converter writes doubles. `tools/check-sources.py` refuses a `float_columns` that names a column already floating, which is how this was found rather than carried over. The two catalogues therefore reach the same numbers by different routes -- text parsed at ingest, and IEEE doubles read straight -- and the 375 rows are byte-identical, which is a cross-check nobody asked for.\n\n`./movesdb/` IS AN UNTRACKED SYMLINK to the monolithic conversion of §7.3.1 (237 tables, 517 MB), kept out of the tree the same way `./esm` is and gitignored beside it. run-tests.sh skips this document's stage when it is absent.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "onroad",
      "domain",
      "default-database"
    ]
  },
